{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: agent/predict.py\n@@\n def ensure_workspace_cwd() -> None:\n@@\n     os.chdir(WORKSPACE)\n+    if not os.environ.get('SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT'):\n+        raise RuntimeError('This frozen campaign requires the offline field-score bundle')\n*** Update File: agent/rank_screen.py\n@@\n-from predict import AGENT_DIR, replay_prompt_scores, parallel_combined\n+from predict import AGENT_DIR, replay_prompt_scores, parallel_combined, ensure_workspace_cwd\n@@\n def main():\n+    ensure_workspace_cwd()\n*** Update File: agent/suggestions.md\n@@\n - Expanded OHLC audit: the July 25 snapshot has real OHLCV/trade-count observations for all seven inspected assets, including Hyperliquid assets, generally at the same density as close. \"'`DATA.md`'\"'s close-only description should be corrected; it can materially misdirect model design. Models must still handle genuinely absent auxiliary fields.\n+- The offline score bundle starts around July 27, 2026, rather than spanning the entire price-history window described in the campaign. Development windows were shifted accordingly.\n+- A single score-only API probe explicitly requested July 1–3, 2026, to check for earlier training history. Although HTTP status was 200, response timestamps violated the requested bounds. The response was rejected before saving or analyzing any CRPS values; no dates or scores from that response were used. Do not rely on server-side historical filtering without a client-side bound check. All evaluation remains on the frozen offline bundle, and evaluators now fail closed if its environment setting is missing.\n*** Update File: agent/journal.md\n@@\n ## Iteration 17 — observed atoms and price discreteness (preregistered)\n@@\n - Lesson: pending.\n+\n+### Historical-score availability safety check\n+\n+One public score-only request explicitly bounded to July 1–3, 2026 returned HTTP 200 but failed the timestamp-bound validator. The response was rejected without storing or studying CRPS values; no out-of-window information was used. Do not repeat or broaden that probe. Continue exclusively with the frozen offline field bundle. The evaluator now refuses to run if \"'`SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is absent. This does not change any CRPS or reward formula.\n+\n+### Full incumbent replay, first development window\n+\n+The neural-plus-0.75-local-cycle model reaches official cumulative Score estimate 16.8173 on dev1, versus 0.32375 for the starter. Reward ranks / ratios to the field'\"'s best: COM_EQU_24H 174/257 and 0.0004981; CRYPTO_1H 136/257 and 0.368394; CRYPTO_24H 118/257 and 0.135626. This is substantial progress but not field leadership, especially for commodities/equities. Dev2 full replay is still running.\n*** Add File: agent/report.md\n+# Better volatility forecasts are not the same as better miner rewards\n+\n+**Status: interim research report. The campaign is active; no champion has been nominated.**\n+\n+## Context\n+\n+Synth SN50 asks a miner for a fan of future prices, not a single price target. This campaign covers all three competitions: five crypto assets at one-minute resolution for one hour, the same assets at five-minute resolution for one day, and eight live commodity/equity assets at five-minute resolution for one day. SPYX is retired and is not modeled or evaluated.\n+\n+Every served prompt must contain the requested number of paths—1000 in the validator—and include the observed starting price. The daily and hourly shapes are therefore 1000 by 289 and 1000 by 61. Prices come only from the frozen snapshot, whose research cutoff is August 29, 2026. Nothing from the subsequent evaluation period is used.\n+\n+The distinction between CRPS and emissions matters. The validator converts per-prompt CRPS into relative prompt scores, applies asset coefficients, smooths scores, and uses a softmax. An arithmetic average of CRPS across assets is not the campaign objective. The campaign's Score is 100 times the mean of the three capped reward ratios to each competition's best field miner.\n+\n+The implementation is anchored to synth-lib commit \"'`782a44fe7585a4e7b0388e22b99c57f13d56f5ee` and its pinned synth-subnet dependency `0d014cbcec376b28e887403b84025ebf1a89f60e`. The source-of-truth functions are `calculate_crps_for_miner`, `compute_prompt_scores`, and `compute_smoothed_score`; scoring and ranking are invoked through `agent/predict.py` and `synth_lib.backtester`, never through a substitute scoring formula.\n+\n+## Approach\n+\n+### Start with trustworthy evaluation\n+\n+The starter'\"'s context loader was strict and rejected legitimate NaNs. It now reads existing parquet partitions directly, preserves missing observations, checks coverage before evaluation, and uses exact archived prompt timestamps. It never repairs missing market prices by interpolation. The current model drops invalid closes and scales returns spanning gaps by the square root of elapsed minutes.\n+\n+The offline field bundle begins around July 27, considerably later than the price history. Development therefore uses **scored times July 28–August 7 and August 8–18**, with August 18–28 reserved for final validation. A daily prompt starts one day before its scored time; the model's fitting cutoff precedes the first development prediction, not merely its score date.\n+\n+The two development windows genuinely differ in volatility. Average daily realized RMS for BTC is approximately 1.50% versus 0.96%; for ETH, 2.06% versus 1.28%; for SP500, 0.71% versus 0.44%; and for AAPLX, 2.12% versus 0.96%. These are more-volatile and calmer periods, not claims about bull and bear markets.\n+\n+### The provisional model\n+\n+The current incumbent uses a robust exponentially weighted absolute-return scale. Its half-life is one hour for hourly forecasts and six hours for daily forecasts. A constant latent volatility multiplier per path gives a Student-like, five-degree-of-freedom log-return mixture. There is no directional log-drift forecast.\n+\n+Daily forecasts additionally use a small pooled neural volatility model. Its 30 inputs summarize eight absolute-return and eight RMS scales, their ratios, the absolute volatility level, and asset identity. A 48/32-unit network predicts a multiplicative volatility correction. Training uses 23,457 contexts before July 1, with 2,508 July 1–25 contexts for chronological model selection. All labels end before July 26. Serving uses exported NumPy weights, not a GPU or a network call.\n+\n+A positive, smoothed hourly volatility profile is estimated from the supplied past seven days and shrunk 25% toward a flat curve. This is **not a market-session model**: there are no opening/closing times, exchange calendars, weekend switches, or zero-volatility hours. The empirical finding is simply that continuous trading does not imply constant variance throughout a day.\n+\n+Timestamp-derived random seeds and shared draws make comparisons reproducible. Representative 1000-path serving checks take a few hundredths of a second, preserve arbitrary positive path counts, tolerate NaN-contaminated contexts, and are invariant to injected rows after the prediction time. Learned weights are not applied before their availability date.\n+\n+## Experiments\n+\n+The journal contains preregistered hypotheses, changes, numerical results, and keep/discard decisions. Important findings so far:\n+\n+| Experiment | Finding |\n+|---|---|\n+| Recent RMS versus absolute-return volatility | Robust recent scale wins consistently; the seven-day starter adapts too slowly. |\n+| One-hour versus six-hour memory | Hourly and daily formats need different memories. A short daily memory overreacts. |\n+| Gaussian versus Student path mixtures | A modest heavy-tailed mixture improves calibration; extreme dispersion does not. |\n+| Five-/15-/30-minute calibration returns | No universal improvement over the minute-based robust estimator. |\n+| Hand-designed volatility reversion | Small, inconsistent gains; not retained. |\n+| Pooled neural forecasting | Material daily gains, but the full hourly correction does not improve mean CRPS. |\n+| IID tails, stochastic volatility, empirical bootstrap | The tested more complicated dynamics do not beat the simple mixture consistently. |\n+| Official-score-curve fine-tuning | 95,750 official training scores produce only a tiny interpolation-loss gain that does not transfer consistently. |\n+| Positive local periodic profile | Improves both daily competitions across both development regimes. |\n+| Moment-matched tail grids | No consistent material improvement over the incumbent mixture. |\n+| Historical profile prior and alternative short memories | Added complexity has negligible or mixed gains. |\n+| Recency-weighted neural refitting | Small, consistent CRPS gains; retained for reward-based confirmation, not automatically adopted. |\n+| Serial return correlation | Estimated correlation is essentially a null result; fixed correlations overfit regimes. |\n+| Direct high/low range substitution | Does not transfer consistently; a fixed diffusion-based range calibration is inadequate. |\n+\n+Two ongoing investigations deserve attention. First, dense hourly reward replays suggest that small mean-CRPS differences can produce large changes in emissions. Second, the snapshot contains nonmissing, internally coherent OHLCV data despite its close-only description. An auxiliary-feature model and discrete-return models are being tested; neither is yet accepted.\n+\n+An implementation failure is included rather than hidden: the first training-label build treated microsecond timestamps as nanoseconds, inflating scale ratios by the square root of 1000. Target-distribution checks caught it before any learned candidate was scored. Those artifacts were discarded, timestamp units were made explicit, and training was rebuilt.\n+\n+## Results\n+\n+Full 1000-path starter replays produce Score estimates **0.32375** and **0.42456** on the two development windows.\n+\n+The full incumbent replay has completed the first window:\n+\n+| Competition | Starter reward rank | Incumbent reward rank | Incumbent reward / best field reward |\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|\n+| COM_EQU_24H | 239 / 257 | 174 / 257 | 0.000498 |\n+| CRYPTO_1H | 216 / 257 | 136 / 257 | 0.368394 |\n+| CRYPTO_24H | 236 / 257 | 118 / 257 | 0.135626 |\n+\n+This corresponds to a Score estimate of **16.8173**, versus 0.32375 for the starter. It is substantial progress, but not field leadership. Commodity/equity emissions remain particularly weak. The second incumbent replay, hourly finalist selection, and the reserved final validation are not yet complete; their results must not be inferred from this table.\n+\n+These are historical reward estimates, not the campaign's eventual post-cutoff Score. Sampled terminal-rank diagnostics and reward-round-thinned screens are explicitly labeled and are not substituted for full final replay.\n+\n+## Limitations\n+\n+- The effective validation sample consists of market days and regimes, not thousands of independent prompt observations. Overlapping horizons create dependence.\n+- Many hypotheses are screened on the same development windows. The reserved final window remains necessary, and small grid gains may be selection noise.\n+- The pinned configuration uses ten-day daily and five-day hourly smoothing windows. Default backtester warmup trimming therefore gives different effective reward intervals; reports must state those intervals rather than repeat the campaign prose's 24-hour description.\n+- The official backtester may replace NaN-holed realized paths with cached validator truth. This behavior is preserved, not silently altered.\n+- A context with no finite positive close cannot supply the required starting price; the model raises a clear error instead of inventing one.\n+- The frozen-data interface does not provide contemporaneous cross-asset information or external news. Additional information used by live miners may not be reproducible here.\n+- A bounded historical score-only API probe returned out-of-request timestamps and was rejected before any score analysis or storage. Evaluation remains strictly offline, with a fail-closed environment check.\n+\n+## What we would do with more budget\n+\n+The campaign still has budget and is continuing. Priorities are to finish emission-sensitive hourly selection, evaluate optional candle/activity information and discrete return distributions, investigate whether a learned lead-time volatility profile adds value, and consider strictly chronological field-aware calibration. Any additional fitted checkpoint must be available before the predictions it evaluates. Final nomination requires a frozen model, a full 1000-path field replay, interface checks, and committed artifacts—not an attractive development-only CRPS table.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\nuv run python -m compileall -q agent\ngit add .gitignore agent/*.py agent/journal.md agent/report.md agent/suggestions.md agent/clock_profiles.* agent/refit_*_weights.* agent/aux_weights.* agent/results/clock_prior_screen.parquet agent/results/hourly_memory_screen.parquet agent/results/matched_shape_screen.parquet agent/results/refit_screen.parquet agent/results/correlation_screen.parquet agent/results/range_screen.parquet agent/results/hourly_emission_screen.parquet agent/results/starter_full/*/*_summary.json agent/results/starter_full/*/competition_metrics.json\ngit -c user.name='Campaign Research Agent' -c user.email='campaign-agent@localhost' commit -m \\\"Record field rewards, safety audits, and extended model experiments\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: agent/predict.py\n@@\n def ensure_workspace_cwd() -> None:\n@@\n     os.chdir(WORKSPACE)\n+    if not os.environ.get('SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT'):\n+        raise RuntimeError('This frozen campaign requires the offline field-score bundle')\n*** Update File: agent/rank_screen.py\n@@\n-from predict import AGENT_DIR, replay_prompt_scores, parallel_combined\n+from predict import AGENT_DIR, replay_prompt_scores, parallel_combined, ensure_workspace_cwd\n@@\n def main():\n+    ensure_workspace_cwd()\n*** Update File: agent/suggestions.md\n@@\n - Expanded OHLC audit: the July 25 snapshot has real OHLCV/trade-count observations for all seven inspected assets, including Hyperliquid assets, generally at the same density as close. \"'`DATA.md`'\"'s close-only description should be corrected; it can materially misdirect model design. Models must still handle genuinely absent auxiliary fields.\n+- The offline score bundle starts around July 27, 2026, rather than spanning the entire price-history window described in the campaign. Development windows were shifted accordingly.\n+- A single score-only API probe explicitly requested July 1–3, 2026, to check for earlier training history. Although HTTP status was 200, response timestamps violated the requested bounds. The response was rejected before saving or analyzing any CRPS values; no dates or scores from that response were used. Do not rely on server-side historical filtering without a client-side bound check. All evaluation remains on the frozen offline bundle, and evaluators now fail closed if its environment setting is missing.\n*** Update File: agent/journal.md\n@@\n ## Iteration 17 — observed atoms and price discreteness (preregistered)\n@@\n - Lesson: pending.\n+\n+### Historical-score availability safety check\n+\n+One public score-only request explicitly bounded to July 1–3, 2026 returned HTTP 200 but failed the timestamp-bound validator. The response was rejected without storing or studying CRPS values; no out-of-window information was used. Do not repeat or broaden that probe. Continue exclusively with the frozen offline field bundle. The evaluator now refuses to run if \"'`SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is absent. This does not change any CRPS or reward formula.\n+\n+### Full incumbent replay, first development window\n+\n+The neural-plus-0.75-local-cycle model reaches official cumulative Score estimate 16.8173 on dev1, versus 0.32375 for the starter. Reward ranks / ratios to the field'\"'s best: COM_EQU_24H 174/257 and 0.0004981; CRYPTO_1H 136/257 and 0.368394; CRYPTO_24H 118/257 and 0.135626. This is substantial progress but not field leadership, especially for commodities/equities. Dev2 full replay is still running.\n*** Add File: agent/report.md\n+# Better volatility forecasts are not the same as better miner rewards\n+\n+**Status: interim research report. The campaign is active; no champion has been nominated.**\n+\n+## Context\n+\n+Synth SN50 asks a miner for a fan of future prices, not a single price target. This campaign covers all three competitions: five crypto assets at one-minute resolution for one hour, the same assets at five-minute resolution for one day, and eight live commodity/equity assets at five-minute resolution for one day. SPYX is retired and is not modeled or evaluated.\n+\n+Every served prompt must contain the requested number of paths—1000 in the validator—and include the observed starting price. The daily and hourly shapes are therefore 1000 by 289 and 1000 by 61. Prices come only from the frozen snapshot, whose research cutoff is August 29, 2026. Nothing from the subsequent evaluation period is used.\n+\n+The distinction between CRPS and emissions matters. The validator converts per-prompt CRPS into relative prompt scores, applies asset coefficients, smooths scores, and uses a softmax. An arithmetic average of CRPS across assets is not the campaign objective. The campaign's Score is 100 times the mean of the three capped reward ratios to each competition's best field miner.\n+\n+The implementation is anchored to synth-lib commit \"'`782a44fe7585a4e7b0388e22b99c57f13d56f5ee` and its pinned synth-subnet dependency `0d014cbcec376b28e887403b84025ebf1a89f60e`. The source-of-truth functions are `calculate_crps_for_miner`, `compute_prompt_scores`, and `compute_smoothed_score`; scoring and ranking are invoked through `agent/predict.py` and `synth_lib.backtester`, never through a substitute scoring formula.\n+\n+## Approach\n+\n+### Start with trustworthy evaluation\n+\n+The starter'\"'s context loader was strict and rejected legitimate NaNs. It now reads existing parquet partitions directly, preserves missing observations, checks coverage before evaluation, and uses exact archived prompt timestamps. It never repairs missing market prices by interpolation. The current model drops invalid closes and scales returns spanning gaps by the square root of elapsed minutes.\n+\n+The offline field bundle begins around July 27, considerably later than the price history. Development therefore uses **scored times July 28–August 7 and August 8–18**, with August 18–28 reserved for final validation. A daily prompt starts one day before its scored time; the model's fitting cutoff precedes the first development prediction, not merely its score date.\n+\n+The two development windows genuinely differ in volatility. Average daily realized RMS for BTC is approximately 1.50% versus 0.96%; for ETH, 2.06% versus 1.28%; for SP500, 0.71% versus 0.44%; and for AAPLX, 2.12% versus 0.96%. These are more-volatile and calmer periods, not claims about bull and bear markets.\n+\n+### The provisional model\n+\n+The current incumbent uses a robust exponentially weighted absolute-return scale. Its half-life is one hour for hourly forecasts and six hours for daily forecasts. A constant latent volatility multiplier per path gives a Student-like, five-degree-of-freedom log-return mixture. There is no directional log-drift forecast.\n+\n+Daily forecasts additionally use a small pooled neural volatility model. Its 30 inputs summarize eight absolute-return and eight RMS scales, their ratios, the absolute volatility level, and asset identity. A 48/32-unit network predicts a multiplicative volatility correction. Training uses 23,457 contexts before July 1, with 2,508 July 1–25 contexts for chronological model selection. All labels end before July 26. Serving uses exported NumPy weights, not a GPU or a network call.\n+\n+A positive, smoothed hourly volatility profile is estimated from the supplied past seven days and shrunk 25% toward a flat curve. This is **not a market-session model**: there are no opening/closing times, exchange calendars, weekend switches, or zero-volatility hours. The empirical finding is simply that continuous trading does not imply constant variance throughout a day.\n+\n+Timestamp-derived random seeds and shared draws make comparisons reproducible. Representative 1000-path serving checks take a few hundredths of a second, preserve arbitrary positive path counts, tolerate NaN-contaminated contexts, and are invariant to injected rows after the prediction time. Learned weights are not applied before their availability date.\n+\n+## Experiments\n+\n+The journal contains preregistered hypotheses, changes, numerical results, and keep/discard decisions. Important findings so far:\n+\n+| Experiment | Finding |\n+|---|---|\n+| Recent RMS versus absolute-return volatility | Robust recent scale wins consistently; the seven-day starter adapts too slowly. |\n+| One-hour versus six-hour memory | Hourly and daily formats need different memories. A short daily memory overreacts. |\n+| Gaussian versus Student path mixtures | A modest heavy-tailed mixture improves calibration; extreme dispersion does not. |\n+| Five-/15-/30-minute calibration returns | No universal improvement over the minute-based robust estimator. |\n+| Hand-designed volatility reversion | Small, inconsistent gains; not retained. |\n+| Pooled neural forecasting | Material daily gains, but the full hourly correction does not improve mean CRPS. |\n+| IID tails, stochastic volatility, empirical bootstrap | The tested more complicated dynamics do not beat the simple mixture consistently. |\n+| Official-score-curve fine-tuning | 95,750 official training scores produce only a tiny interpolation-loss gain that does not transfer consistently. |\n+| Positive local periodic profile | Improves both daily competitions across both development regimes. |\n+| Moment-matched tail grids | No consistent material improvement over the incumbent mixture. |\n+| Historical profile prior and alternative short memories | Added complexity has negligible or mixed gains. |\n+| Recency-weighted neural refitting | Small, consistent CRPS gains; retained for reward-based confirmation, not automatically adopted. |\n+| Serial return correlation | Estimated correlation is essentially a null result; fixed correlations overfit regimes. |\n+| Direct high/low range substitution | Does not transfer consistently; a fixed diffusion-based range calibration is inadequate. |\n+\n+Two ongoing investigations deserve attention. First, dense hourly reward replays suggest that small mean-CRPS differences can produce large changes in emissions. Second, the snapshot contains nonmissing, internally coherent OHLCV data despite its close-only description. An auxiliary-feature model and discrete-return models are being tested; neither is yet accepted.\n+\n+An implementation failure is included rather than hidden: the first training-label build treated microsecond timestamps as nanoseconds, inflating scale ratios by the square root of 1000. Target-distribution checks caught it before any learned candidate was scored. Those artifacts were discarded, timestamp units were made explicit, and training was rebuilt.\n+\n+## Results\n+\n+Full 1000-path starter replays produce Score estimates **0.32375** and **0.42456** on the two development windows.\n+\n+The full incumbent replay has completed the first window:\n+\n+| Competition | Starter reward rank | Incumbent reward rank | Incumbent reward / best field reward |\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|\n+| COM_EQU_24H | 239 / 257 | 174 / 257 | 0.000498 |\n+| CRYPTO_1H | 216 / 257 | 136 / 257 | 0.368394 |\n+| CRYPTO_24H | 236 / 257 | 118 / 257 | 0.135626 |\n+\n+This corresponds to a Score estimate of **16.8173**, versus 0.32375 for the starter. It is substantial progress, but not field leadership. Commodity/equity emissions remain particularly weak. The second incumbent replay, hourly finalist selection, and the reserved final validation are not yet complete; their results must not be inferred from this table.\n+\n+These are historical reward estimates, not the campaign's eventual post-cutoff Score. Sampled terminal-rank diagnostics and reward-round-thinned screens are explicitly labeled and are not substituted for full final replay.\n+\n+## Limitations\n+\n+- The effective validation sample consists of market days and regimes, not thousands of independent prompt observations. Overlapping horizons create dependence.\n+- Many hypotheses are screened on the same development windows. The reserved final window remains necessary, and small grid gains may be selection noise.\n+- The pinned configuration uses ten-day daily and five-day hourly smoothing windows. Default backtester warmup trimming therefore gives different effective reward intervals; reports must state those intervals rather than repeat the campaign prose's 24-hour description.\n+- The official backtester may replace NaN-holed realized paths with cached validator truth. This behavior is preserved, not silently altered.\n+- A context with no finite positive close cannot supply the required starting price; the model raises a clear error instead of inventing one.\n+- The frozen