{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_307","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md && printf '\\\\n--- BUDGET ---\\\\n' && cat agent/BUDGET.md && printf '\\\\n--- JOURNAL ---\\\\n' && tail -n 140 agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_307","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md && printf '\\\\n--- BUDGET ---\\\\n' && cat agent/BUDGET.md && printf '\\\\n--- JOURNAL ---\\\\n' && tail -n 140 agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"# CAMPAIGN campaign-3 — constitution (gpt-6-astra)\n\nYou are an autonomous research agent. Nobody is watching. NEVER ask a question.\nNEVER stop before the landing order. Your mission:\n\n> Improve the Synth SN50 miner: produce the best possible price-simulation model as measured by CRPS. Your champion will be evaluated on the 10 days following your data cutoff, across the three competitions (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H), against the real field of miners, using the validator's weighted ranking — your Score is your simulated emissions relative to the field's best miner (see the constitution). Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The competitions you're targeting\n\nThree competitions, two formats. Your champion is evaluated on all three — a `simulate()` that\nignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon will fail on crypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n**How you'll be ranked.** The validator does not average across assets: it aggregates a\ncompetition's scores using **per-asset coefficients** (`ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` in\n`synth.validator.moving_average`), normalizes per miner, then applies a softmax — an asset with\na high coefficient weighs far more heavily in the ranking. \n\n**How you'll be SCORED (pre-registered — design for this).** After the campaign your champion\nis injected into the archived field as one more miner over the **10 days following your data\ncutoff** and run through the validator's own aggregation. Your Score is\n**100 × mean over the 3 competitions of min(1, your total reward weight ÷ the top miner's)** —\nsimulated emissions relative to the field's best. Its consequences:\n- Each competition is worth 1/3 of the Score, regardless of asset count.\n- The 10-day window matches the validator's 24h smoothing window, so your rank is steady-state\n— no new-miner penalty. Estimate this Score for your own candidates with the official\n  backtester before you nominate.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py` is also used for the 1h format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment\n  60`).\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-08-29**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index), trading ~24/7 with NO\n  overnight or weekend sessions: do not build a session-of-day structure that does not exist.\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs ranking**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 competition split (breaks table below) floors only FIELD-RELATIVE RANKS — never\n  compute a rank on a window that starts before it.\n\n  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact function and ranks you against the real field of miners. Use it via\n  `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement your own scoring. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE\n  here: `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle\n  of historical field scores and rewards inside your snapshot — you do not need the API for\n  ranks, and the bundle covers your whole data window.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; dissecting the paths served by an individual miner in order to\n  copy them is FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, dissecting an\n  individual miner's served paths to copy them is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't beat closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n\n## Hard rules (violations = disqualification)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-08-29** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-08-29 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   disqualifies you. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS/rank evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). This is what makes your work comparable to others'.\n4. Forbidden: falsifying the journal/results; fetching or dissecting predictions served by\n   individual miners.\n5. **ABSOLUTE prohibition on reading the operator's own private research code** (any\n   already-developed miner model outside your workspace). It should not exist anywhere in your\n   environment. If you come across it by any means whatsoever — a mount, a copy, an archive, a\n   search result — do not access it, do not read it, do not draw on it, and report it in\n   `suggestions.md`. Your work must start from public sources (synth-lib, synth-subnet, the\n   Synth API) and your own ideas.\n6. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested rank per competition,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths — a nomination\n  without field-relative numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n\n--- BUDGET ---\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 44.56 / 100.00 USD (45%)\n- Time   : 2.8 h elapsed, 15.2 h remaining (15%)\n- Phase  : normal\n\n--- JOURNAL ---\n\n### Iteration 10 result\n\nMoment matching does not reveal a consistent material improvement. Student df3 at matched absolute scale 1.0 gives hourly 0.99920/0.99939 but daily-crypto 1.00044/1.00166; other scale choices trade gains in the calmer window for losses in the earlier window. Retain df5 at the existing scale. This confirms that the prior simple mixture was near a broad calibration optimum; it does not prove all heavy-tailed models are equivalent.\n\n## Iteration 11 — periodic-profile stability (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: six or seven observed days make the local variance profile noisy. A strictly pre-field historical profile can regularize it without any assumed exchange calendar or zero-variance periods.\n- Change: fit positive per-asset hourly profiles from April 1 (or first trade) through July 26, using the same within-day normalization and circular smoothing. Blend 25/50/75/100% historical prior into the local profile; keep total cycle strength 0.75. Separately check 15/30/60/120-minute robust half-lives for hourly forecasts, changing nothing else.\n- Result: pending official development-window CRPS. The historical profile never reads validation-period prices when fitted.\n- Verdict: require consistent gains across both windows; otherwise prefer the local-only profile and 60-minute hourly scale.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 11 result\n\nKeep local-only profile and 60-minute hourly memory. Historical profile shrinkage has <0.1% gains and mixed COM_EQU performance (100% prior 0.99930/1.00120). Hourly 15/30/120-minute half-lives versus 60-minute produce 1.00186/1.00422, 1.00008/1.00028, and 1.00135/1.00211. The additional parameters are not justified.\n\n## Iteration 12 — chronological refitting and recency weighting (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the original network's optimizer only fitted labels before July 1; July 1–25 was used for model selection. Refitting on all labels already available before the field windows, with recency weighting, may better match newer venues without violating no-lookahead.\n- Change: initialize from the likelihood model, make 40 fixed-epoch passes over all pre-July-26 labels, and compare uniform versus 60/120-day exponential recency weights. No development or final-window labels enter the fit. Daily correction remains full strength with the 0.75 local profile; hourly stays classical. Compare optional half-strength shrinkage only if full corrections regress.\n- Result: pending official development-window CRPS. Training-batch likelihood is only diagnostic, not evidence of improvement.\n- Verdict: keep only a consistent cross-window gain; otherwise retain the original frozen weights.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Full baseline reward replay\n\nThe 1000-path deterministic starter has official cumulative-emission Score estimates 0.32375 on dev1 and 0.42456 on dev2. Reward ranks (COM_EQU_24H / CRYPTO_1H / CRYPTO_24H) are 239/216/236 of 257 on dev1 and 248/227/257 on dev2. These are full archived-prompt, all-asset replays, not the earlier sampled terminal-rank diagnostics. The two concepts must not be conflated.\n\n### Evaluation throughput change\n\nReward replay is now optionally partitioned by reward timestamps across workers, each calling the unchanged official `compute_combined_smoothed_scores` with the full score history; the unchanged official warmup trim is applied again after concatenation. Verify equality with the serial implementation, including a split across warmup, before using it for nomination. This is parallel scheduling, not a new score/rank formula.\n\n### Iteration 12 result\n\nRecency refitting gives small but directionally consistent gains. The 60-day weighted refit gives COM_EQU_24H 0.99942/0.99812 and CRYPTO_24H 0.99926/0.99901 versus the current neural-cycle incumbent. These are below the original 0.5% CRPS screen threshold, so do not replace the weights yet. Because emissions are nonlinear, retain this as a candidate for coefficient-weighted reward confirmation rather than treating the threshold as proof of no utility.\n\n## Iteration 13 — cross-horizon return dependence (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: even a well-calibrated one-step distribution can misprice longer scored intervals if returns are not independent. Mild serial dependence may improve multi-horizon CRPS without changing one-step marginal scale.\n- Change: preserve the full neural-plus-local-cycle incumbent and df5 marginal mixture; generate stationary AR(1) Gaussian innovations at correlations -0.2/-0.1/+0.1/+0.2, plus a half-shrunk correlation estimated from valid adjacent context returns at the served step size. No price interpolation, drift signal, or future information is used.\n- Result: pending official development-window CRPS.\n- Verdict: require consistent cross-window improvement; otherwise retain independent increments.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 13 result\n\nReject serial correlation as a general improvement. Half-shrunk empirical correlation gives ratios within 0.02% of the independent model; fixed nonzero correlations trade gains between windows and horizons. For example rho=-0.1 yields hourly 1.00132/1.00298, whereas rho=+0.1 yields 1.00069/0.99878. The data-driven estimate is effectively a null result, and introducing a fixed correlation would be tuning noise.\n\n### Parallel replay verification\n\nSerial and three-worker official combined replay are exactly equal across all columns on 1,542 result rows, with input reward times straddling the hourly warmup boundary. The scheduling optimization is validated.\n\n## Iteration 14 — emission-sensitive hourly selection (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the softmax can make small, consistent CRPS gains economically relevant even when they fall below a generic 0.5% screen gate. The remaining hourly candidates should be compared on archived reward aggregation, not discarded solely on unweighted CRPS averages.\n- Change: score every archived hourly prompt in both development windows at 1000 paths for the incumbent, moment-matched df3, half-strength ridge/neural/score-fitted corrections, 30-minute memory, mild volatility reversion, and +/-3% scale adjustments. Reuse only reward timestamps from the full baseline backtest; recompute prompt scores and combined weights using the official functions. Thin reward rounds by a fixed factor for initial selection, then replay every round for finalists.\n- Result: pending. All candidates were specified before this dense comparison; no final-window observations enter selection.\n- Verdict: promote only a candidate whose reward-ratio gain is consistent across both windows and survives full-round replay. Report this adjusted selection criterion transparently.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n## Iteration 15 — use available intraminute information (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: close-to-close volatility throws away intraminute range information, which may improve short-horizon scale estimates. The snapshot documentation incorrectly describes non-close columns as empty.\n- Change: direct inspection on July 25 shows usable OHLCV/trade counts for BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP (100% of minutes), HYPE/SP500 (~99.4%), and NVDAX (~53.6%, matching its close density that day). Compute a recent mean log(high/low) scale and geometrically blend it with the close-based scale. Compare 50/100% blend and 0.9/1.0/1.1 range calibration. If high/low are absent or unusable, fall back to the close-only forecast. No online prices are used.\n- Result: pending official CRPS on both development windows. A direct blend is a diagnostic; retraining with range information would be a separate experiment.\n- Verdict: require consistent gains and robustness to NaN/absent OHLC inputs.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 15 result and candle audit\n\nReject direct range substitution. A 50% range blend at calibration 1.0 yields COM_EQU_24H 1.01133/0.99371, hourly 1.00323/1.00662, and daily crypto 1.00361/0.99953. The range scale is much smaller than the close-based scale (e.g. 0.635 for BTC and 0.540 for AAPLX on the July-25 context). This is not evidence that OHLC is corrupted: all inspected complete candles satisfy low <= open/close <= high; BTC's open is essentially the previous close, while sparse Hyperliquid assets have material between-candle opening moves. Naive continuous-diffusion range calibration is not sufficient here.\n\n## Iteration 16 — learn from optional candle and activity features (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: a learned model can use range/close discrepancies and changes in volume/trade intensity without forcing an inappropriate fixed range calibration.\n- Change: add 19 range, coverage, volume, and trade-count features to the 30 existing inputs. Initialize from the close-only network with zero weights on the new inputs; preserve the original pre-July-1 training / July-1–25 selection split and 150-epoch cap. Compare full and half-strength auxiliary corrections on both development windows. Use the incumbent when auxiliary data are unusable.\n- Result: pending. Optional-field availability is checked explicitly; no model may depend on interpolated or invented OHLCV values.\n- Verdict: require out-of-sample official CRPS/emission gains, not just better training likelihood.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n## Iteration 17 — observed atoms and price discreteness (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: a continuous diffusion misses the atom at zero in observed returns. The earlier empirical bootstrap subtracted its sample mean and normalized RMS, which both obscured this question.\n- Change: test a symmetrized empirical step-return bootstrap normalized by mean absolute magnitude, a zero-inflated increment model using past valid zero-return frequency, and simulated-price quantization at the smallest reliable observed price increment. None treats NaN as zero or assumes a closed trading session. Keep the same neural scale and positive local volatility profile.\n- Result: pending. A pre-field July-25 context has zero-return fractions (1m/5m) BTC 3.7%/0.2%, SOL 14.2%/6.4%, XAU 27.8%/11.3%, and SP500 12.2%/5.2%; these motivate a measured test, not a presumed gain.\n- Verdict: retain only consistent official CRPS/reward improvements, with arbitrary path-count and positive-price contracts preserved.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Historical-score availability safety check\n\nOne public score-only request explicitly bounded to July 1–3, 2026 returned HTTP 200 but failed the timestamp-bound validator. The response was rejected without storing or studying CRPS values; no out-of-window information was used. Do not repeat or broaden that probe. Continue exclusively with the frozen offline field bundle. The evaluator now refuses to run if `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is absent. This does not change any CRPS or reward formula.\n\n### Full incumbent replay, first development window\n\nThe neural-plus-0.75-local-cycle model reaches official cumulative Score estimate 16.8173 on dev1, versus 0.32375 for the starter. Reward ranks / ratios to the field's best: COM_EQU_24H 174/257 and 0.0004981; CRYPTO_1H 136/257 and 0.368394; CRYPTO_24H 118/257 and 0.135626. This is substantial progress but not field leadership, especially for commodities/equities. Dev2 full replay is still running.\n\n### Full incumbent replay, second development window\n\nDev2 finishes at Score estimate 16.1181 versus starter 0.42456. Reward ranks / ratios are COM_EQU_24H 231/257 and 0.00015596; CRYPTO_1H 198/257 and 0.446012; CRYPTO_24H 230/257 and 0.0373755. The model improves both regimes, but the remaining daily gap is large.\n\n### Iteration 16 and 17 results\n\nThe auxiliary network does not improve the chronological likelihood diagnostic (best 1.019339 versus 1.019301 for close-only), and its official CRPS gains are tiny: full auxiliary daily ratios COM_EQU 0.99944/0.99990 and crypto 0.99975/0.99981. Keep it experimental, not deployed. Symmetric empirical bootstrap and zero inflation also fail the consistency gate. Price quantization has small consistent gains (hourly 0.99947/0.99911), so retain it only for later reward-based confirmation.\n\n## Iteration 18 — align training with the actual prompt-score rule (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the previous score-curve fit divided each prompt by its realized baseline CRPS, inadvertently giving different weights to different future outcomes. The actual pinned validator does not do that: valid prompt scores are raw CRPS minus the prompt minimum. The 95th percentile is used only to fill missed responses; valid scores are neither capped nor divided by a percentile range. Legacy helper names/comments are misleading, so the executed `compute_prompt_scores` source is decisive.\n- Change: reuse the same official CRPS response curves, network, split, and optimizer, but weight raw scores by the validator asset coefficients and balance historical asset sample counts. Normalize only by a constant training-set scale per competition for numerical conditioning. This avoids outcome-dependent per-prompt normalization. No additional field history is needed, because a common prompt-minimum subtraction cancels in complete-coverage miner comparisons.\n- Result: pending fresh official development-window CRPS and rewards. Compare full and half-strength corrections against a tuned incumbent with the promising half-strength hourly neural correction.\n- Verdict: adopt only if both development windows and full reward replays support the change.\n- Lesson: pending; this experiment corrects an objective mismatch, not the official scorer.\n\n### Iteration 2 half-life result\n\nThe 1h half-life beats 6h for crypto-hourly in both development windows: ratios 0.9935 and 0.9870. The same change hurts daily crypto (1.0083/1.0049) and daily commodities/equities (1.0143/1.0094). Keep 1h half-life for hourly, 6h for daily. This is a horizon-specific result, not an asset-specific fitted exception. Next isolate scale and path-mixture effects around this `auto` baseline.\n\n### Iteration 2 mixture result\n\nAt 256 paths, a five-degree Student variance mixture with unchanged RMS scale improves mean relative CRPS versus auto-Gaussian: COM_EQU_24H 0.9966/0.9876, CRYPTO_1H 0.9964/0.9986, CRYPTO_24H 0.9982/0.9953 (dev1/dev2). Increasing Gaussian scale hurts every competition; reducing by 10% has mixed gains. Keep df=5 provisionally, but the sub-0.5% hourly gains require denser and 1000-path confirmation. HYPE does not benefit on the pooled screen; do not introduce an asset exception without further evidence.\n\n## Iteration 3 — sampling frequency (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: minute-scale microstructure and illiquidity can distort the diffusion estimate for daily five-minute paths. Estimating scale from actual 5/15/30-minute differences may better match scored horizons.\n- Change: retain identical auto half-life and df=5 mixture; vary only the return lag used for scale estimation. Missing endpoint pairs are dropped, never filled or interpolated.\n- Result: pending official CRPS screen on both development windows.\n- Verdict: pending; require a consistent >0.5% gain or reject extra complexity.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 1 result\n\nOfficial CRPS screening uses 60 prompts per asset/format/window (2160 prompts total), 256 paths, five candidates. Six-hour absolute-return scale versus deterministic starter: mean asset-relative CRPS ratios are dev1/dev2 0.9750/0.9002 for COM_EQU_24H, 0.9729/0.9438 for CRYPTO_1H, and 0.9933/0.9761 for CRYPTO_24H. It is the best hourly variant for all five crypto assets in both windows. Keep robust recent scale as the next baseline. The windows differ materially in calibration improvement, although this alone does not establish opposite market regimes. Full official 1000-path field replays are starting; reduced-path averages are not emission estimates.\n\n## Iteration 2 — scale and mixture calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: absolute-return scaling still understates the peaked, heavy-tailed return distribution. A pathwise variance mixture can improve both near-zero sharpness and tail coverage without a directional forecast.\n- Change: screen 1h/3h/6h/12h absolute-return half-lives, then multiplier and Student-t path-scale grids against av:360. Do not change multiple dimensions in the first half-life experiment.\n- Result: pending; retain candidates only if both development windows improve by at least 0.5% or a competition improves without a >1% regression elsewhere.\n- Verdict: pending.\n- Lesson: pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_308","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; find . -name AGENTS.md -print; head -n 110 agent/journal.md; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_308","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; find . -name AGENTS.md -print; head -n 110 agent/journal.md; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"# CAMPAIGN campaign-3 — constitution (gpt-6-astra)\n\nYou are an autonomous research agent. Nobody is watching. NEVER ask a question.\nNEVER stop before the landing order. Your mission:\n\n> Improve the Synth SN50 miner: produce the best possible price-simulation model as measured by CRPS. Your champion will be evaluated on the 10 days following your data cutoff, across the three competitions (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H), against the real field of miners, using the validator's weighted ranking — your Score is your simulated emissions relative to the field's best miner (see the constitution). Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The competitions you're targeting\n\nThree competitions, two formats. Your champion is evaluated on all three — a `simulate()` that\nignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon will fail on crypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n**How you'll be ranked.** The validator does not average across assets: it aggregates a\ncompetition's scores using **per-asset coefficients** (`ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` in\n`synth.validator.moving_average`), normalizes per miner, then applies a softmax — an asset with\na high coefficient weighs far more heavily in the ranking. \n\n**How you'll be SCORED (pre-registered — design for this).** After the campaign your champion\nis injected into the archived field as one more miner over the **10 days following your data\ncutoff** and run through the validator's own aggregation. Your Score is\n**100 × mean over the 3 competitions of min(1, your total reward weight ÷ the top miner's)** —\nsimulated emissions relative to the field's best. Its consequences:\n- Each competition is worth 1/3 of the Score, regardless of asset count.\n- The 10-day window matches the validator's 24h smoothing window, so your rank is steady-state\n— no new-miner penalty. Estimate this Score for your own candidates with the official\n  backtester before you nominate.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py` is also used for the 1h format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment\n  60`).\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-08-29**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index), trading ~24/7 with NO\n  overnight or weekend sessions: do not build a session-of-day structure that does not exist.\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs ranking**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 competition split (breaks table below) floors only FIELD-RELATIVE RANKS — never\n  compute a rank on a window that starts before it.\n\n  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact function and ranks you against the real field of miners. Use it via\n  `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement your own scoring. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE\n  here: `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle\n  of historical field scores and rewards inside your snapshot — you do not need the API for\n  ranks, and the bundle covers your whole data window.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; dissecting the paths served by an individual miner in order to\n  copy them is FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, dissecting an\n  individual miner's served paths to copy them is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't beat closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n\n## Hard rules (violations = disqualification)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-08-29** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-08-29 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   disqualifies you. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS/rank evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). This is what makes your work comparable to others'.\n4. Forbidden: falsifying the journal/results; fetching or dissecting predictions served by\n   individual miners.\n5. **ABSOLUTE prohibition on reading the operator's own private research code** (any\n   already-developed miner model outside your workspace). It should not exist anywhere in your\n   environment. If you come across it by any means whatsoever — a mount, a copy, an archive, a\n   search result — do not access it, do not read it, do not draw on it, and report it in\n   `suggestions.md`. Your work must start from public sources (synth-lib, synth-subnet, the\n   Synth API) and your own ideas.\n6. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested rank per competition,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths — a nomination\n  without field-relative numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 44.56 / 100.00 USD (45%)\n- Time   : 2.8 h elapsed, 15.2 h remaining (15%)\n- Phase  : normal\n# Research journal — campaign-3\n\n### Full daily refinement confirmation and promotion\n\nThe full-temporal/Sobol stack with a 30-minute, 0.75-strength crypto clock reaches daily-crypto reward ratios 0.218222/0.126951, ranks 62/163 out of 257. A 15-minute, full-strength COM_EQU clock reaches 0.014075/0.009365, ranks 129/216. Both improve the earlier lead-only daily model in both windows at 1000 paths and all reward rounds. The half-temporal crypto alternative gives 0.249621/0.099636: a marginally higher two-window mean, but a substantially weaker minimum-window result. Prefer the common full temporal strength for robustness and fewer competition-specific strength parameters; do not claim it maximizes the two-window arithmetic mean. Promote the full stack for both daily competitions. Together with the confirmed hourly model, development Score estimates are approximately 36.724 and 33.618.\n\n## Iteration 33 — chronological checkpoint refresh (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: additional completed labels from late July/early August may improve calibration on newer venues more than context-only bias correction did.\n- Change: append snapshot-only training rows with every target ending before August 7, then repeat the already tested fixed 40-epoch, 60-day-recency fine-tune from the original static network. Compare against both the unchanged strong stack and the old-data 40-epoch fine-tune, at half/full strengths. Keep temporal weights and path construction fixed. Activate either fine-tune no earlier than August 7; consequently dev1 is unchanged by construction and only dev2 measures refresh effects. No final-window prices or labels enter this experiment.\n- Result: pending. This is a one-window causal refresh diagnostic, not two independent confirmations. A successful policy would require a separately frozen rolling-origin validation before final nomination.\n- Verdict: require improvement over the old-data control, not just over the unfine-tuned baseline; otherwise do not attribute any gain to fresher labels.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n## Iteration 32 — price-lattice projection on the hourly finalist (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: projecting continuous fans onto a price grid inferred from observed quotes may improve calibration at the scored price precision. The earlier quantization experiment used a weaker hourly model and did not receive final reward confirmation.\n- Change: preserve the exact-temporal/Sobol hourly forecast and compare two past-context grid estimators: minimum positive close difference, and a greatest-common-divisor lattice after controlled decimal conversion. Project only generated prices, never repair or round historical market data. Do not choose a new random seed or change volatility.\n- Result: pending dense 1000-path official CRPS and rewards in both development windows.\n- Verdict: require consistent gains and verify inferred-grid safety; otherwise retain continuous paths.\n- Lesson: pending. A minimum observed price change is not automatically the exchange tick size, so the estimators are explicit hypotheses.\n\n### Iteration 31 result\n\nContext-only adaptive calibration does not pass the consistency gate. Quarter-strength gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 1.00027/0.99849, hourly 1.00064/1.00012, and daily crypto 1.00002/0.99932; half strength increases regressions. Reject. The completed-volatility moment ratio is not a reliable replacement for the fixed conditional forecast.\n\n### Fine-grid calibration development result\n\nThe 300-epoch fine-grid correction still fails across regimes: half strength gives COM_EQU 0.99864/1.00152, hourly 1.00003/0.99980, daily crypto 0.99968/1.00222. Full strength is worse in dev2. Reject this checkpoint. The independently preregistered 600-epoch run selects epoch 388 (pre-field objective 0.799594), with its development screen pending; no further extensions are planned.\n\n### Fine-grid convergence check (before development inspection)\n\nThe 1000-path fine-grid fit improves the pre-field objective from 0.800481 to 0.799637, with its best epoch at the fixed limit of 299. Before inspecting its new development results, preregister one extension to 600 epochs with all other settings unchanged, preserving the 300-epoch artifact. If the selected epoch remains below 300, skip duplicate development scoring. No further epoch extensions are planned for this experiment.\n\n### Updated serving audit\n\nThe promoted hourly implementation imports no Torch and remains self-contained. Production/research paths match to relative tolerance 1e-12 after aligning floating-point multiplication order; an initial approximately 1e-11 discrepancy was numerical rather than a model difference. Representative 1000-path calls take 0.028–0.047 seconds hourly and 0.059–0.062 seconds daily. NaN-contaminated contexts, 17 requested paths, and injected future rows pass the shape, positivity, finiteness and invariance checks.\n\n### Full exact-temporal/Sobol hourly confirmation and promotion\n\nAt every official reward round and 1000 paths, full exact-context temporal correction plus direct scrambled Sobol improves hourly reward ratios from 0.629991 to 0.869436 in dev1 and from 0.704092 to 0.872234 in dev2. Cumulative ranks improve 87→25 and 135→14 out of 257. Final-round ranks are 60 and 1, respectively; the latter is not the cumulative rank. Promote these hourly components with self-contained NumPy/SciPy inference in `modeling.py`. Daily remains the confirmed lead-shaped model pending its own finer-clock confirmation. Development Score estimates are now approximately 34.052 and 31.044; the reserved final window is still untouched.\n\n## Iteration 31 — context-only adaptive calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: a fixed pooled model can retain a slow-moving calibration bias on newer venues. Recently completed volatility forecasts inside the supplied seven-day context may identify that bias without retraining or external data.\n- Change: reconstruct nine completed static-neural volatility forecasts using only observations available at each historical anchor, compare their predicted scales with subsequently observed absolute-return scales, and shrink the ratio of means by strengths 0.25/0.5. Hourly anchors cover the past nine completed hours; daily anchors span the past three days with six-hour spacing. Skip anchors preceding weight availability and require at least three usable forecasts. Cap the unshrunk ratio to [0.5,2].\n- Result: pending official development CRPS against the lead-shaped incumbent. Overlapping daily labels are not treated as independent observations. Historical subcontexts are necessarily shorter than seven days and this approximation must be reported.\n- Verdict: retain only if adaptation improves both regimes without destabilizing other competitions; otherwise prefer the fixed forecaster.\n- Lesson: pending. Every calibration target ends by the actual prediction time, and no past served miner paths are used.\n\n### Iteration 30 result and bounded refinement\n\nThe shape-matched experiment computes 47,875 official CRPS values at 1000 paths over 6,081 contexts. No fitted epoch improves the pre-development validation objective of 0.800481; the selector retains the exact zero correction. Reject this fit without redundant development scoring. One bounded follow-up is preregistered: resolve the scale grid more finely around 1, because a coarse piecewise-linear surrogate can place its minimum at a grid knot even when the underlying smooth expected score has a nearby optimum. Preserve the original artifact and do not call the interpolated objective an official CRPS evaluation.\n\n## Iteration 30 — shape-matched official-score calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the previous score-curve fit used a flat classical path family at 128 paths, while deployment now includes learned scale and clock/lead profiles. Training and inference objectives therefore differed even after correcting raw-score weighting.\n- Change: recompute five scale-response points around the lead-shaped incumbent at 1000 paths on the same pre-field training contexts, using the official scorer and its realized-path slicing helper. Fit a small residual log-scale network, initialized to no correction, using raw asset-weighted CRPS and constant competition-level numerical scaling. Keep the existing chronological split and bounded corrections.\n- Result: pending. These are in-sample/meta-training response curves around fitted parameters, not historical causal backtests; only later development-window evaluations are performance claims. No field-rank calculation uses these training dates.\n- Verdict: require consistent development CRPS and official reward gains before adopting a calibration correction.\n- Lesson: pending; fixing objective alignment is necessary to test an idea, not evidence that it will win.\n\n## Iteration 29 — finer-clock interaction and joint normalization (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: finer empirical clocks may complement the temporal residual. Separately, normalizing clock and lead components individually does not guarantee that their product has unit mean; the earlier description of perfectly preserved average scale was too strong.\n- Change: around full exact-temporal plus half-lead, compare 30-/15-minute clocks, full clock strength at 15-minute resolution, and explicit unit-mean normalization of the final product. Each comparison has an adjacent one-variable control. All forecasts remain positive and continuous-trading, with no sessions or zero-volatility gates.\n- Result: a 16-context diagnostic finds joint mean multipliers from 0.9992 to 1.0252 despite unit-mean components. Existing held-out forecasts and rewards remain valid; only the previous interpretation needs correction. The simpler clock screen gives consistent equity gains at 30-/15-minute resolution (ratios 0.99761/0.99799 and 0.99659/0.99745). Crypto favors the more conservative 30-minute profile (0.99929/0.999994). Lowering the floor alone is effectively a null change in this sample.\n- Verdict: adopt only on fresh official CRPS and reward evidence, not merely because unit-mean normalization sounds cleaner.\n- Lesson: component normalizations do not commute with multiplication. Document the actual forecast, not the intended interpretation.\n\n## Iteration 28 — empirical clock resolution and shrinkage (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the current hourly clock and three-bin smoothing may blur repeatable volatility bursts. Its 25% profile floor and 25% shrinkage also enforce substantial dispersion in empirically quiet periods. These are modeling choices, not venue constraints.\n- Change: against the lead-shaped incumbent, change one feature at a time: 30-minute versus hourly bins, 15-minute versus hourly bins, full versus 0.75 profile strength, or a 0.05 versus 0.25 positive profile floor. Keep the same within-day normalization, smoothing kernel, maximum profile value and seven-day context. Every bin remains strictly positive; no trading sessions, closures or external calendars are introduced.\n- Result: pending both development windows. A score-only field diagnostic shows the largest weighted daily gaps are AAPLX, TSLAX, GOOGLX and NVDAX, not SP500 despite its higher coefficient. This diagnostic compares public aggregate score levels, never miners' served paths.\n- Verdict: require consistent official CRPS and reward gains before combining with the temporal candidate.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 27 result\n\nForecast-error mixtures fail the consistency gate at 1000 paths. Half conditional replacement gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 0.99987/1.00065, hourly 1.00007/1.00038, daily crypto 1.00015/1.00099. Full replacement regresses further, and conditioning on the recent volatility trend barely differs from pooling. Discard these mixtures. The simpler Student mixture remains better calibrated than this empirical future-volatility uncertainty proxy.\n\n## Iteration 27 — forecast-error volatility mixtures (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: uncertainty about future average volatility may differ by horizon and recent volatility trend; a fixed Student scale mixture ignores this structure.\n- Change: pool pre-July-1 ratios of future realized absolute-return scale to the static forecast, separately by crypto/noncrypto and horizon, optionally conditioning on falling/stable/rising recent volatility. Store 101 positive empirical quantiles, normalize each pool to the same expected path multiplier as the df5 incumbent, then replace half/all latent Student scales with these forecast-error scales. This changes distribution shape, not mean absolute movement, and does not bootstrap raw price returns.\n- Result: pending 1000-path official CRPS screens in both development windows.\n- Verdict: retain only a consistent gain; otherwise keep the simpler Student mixture. The temporal and periodic mean-scale models remain separate experiments.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n## Iteration 26 — smooth data-fitted periodic scale (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: continuously traded markets can have smooth activity cycles even without sessions or closures. Past-week clock shape does not explicitly forecast how average future volatility depends on the current phase of those cycles.\n- Change: fit a strongly regularized positive scale residual from daily/weekly sine and cosine harmonics, shared and interacted with asset identity. Fit only the pre-July-1 rows and select regularization on July 1–25; use the confirmed half-hourly/full-daily neural baseline. Bound log corrections at +/-0.5 and test half/full strengths. There are no exchange calendars, market-hour gates, weekend exclusions, or zero-volatility bins.\n- Result: pending chronological likelihood and official development CRPS against the lead-shaped incumbent.\n- Verdict: require consistent gains; reject rather than invent a trading-session explanation if the data do not support this periodic predictor.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n### Iteration 25 result\n\nAll three ridge penalties worsen chronological validation mean-square error versus zero location: baseline 1.82452; penalties 100/1000/10000 give 1.84238/1.84066/1.83205 on 2,208 validation rows (20,215 training rows). The selector correctly exports zero coefficients. Reject before spending on redundant CRPS screens, because every proposed location path would equal the incumbent. Keep zero log drift; no evidence supports a directional adjustment.\n\n### Temporal boundary audit and exact-context refit\n\nThe cached temporal training sequence includes one return at the seven-day left boundary that runtime cannot form from its supplied frame. Three sampled contexts show differences only in the first hourly bin: maximum 0.0167–0.0284, with every later bin exactly equal. This is older-context mismatch, not future leakage; all development predictions already used the exact runtime context. Nonetheless, refit a separately named `temporal_exact` artifact using the runtime feature function for every training row before promotion. Preserve the original artifact/results rather than silently changing their provenance.\n\n## Iteration 25 — conditional price location (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: zero conditional log drift may leave small mean-reversion or momentum information unused. This is distinct from the previous experiments on volatility reversion and serial covariance.\n- Change: fit a heavily regularized ridge model for standardized future log returns at 5, 60 and 1440 minutes, from the original static inputs plus ten trailing signed-return horizons and their availability indicators. Select the ridge penalty only on the pre-July-26 chronological holdout, compare to a zero forecast, cap each predicted location at 0.3 reference standard deviations, and test quarter/half strengths against the promoted lead-shaped incumbent. No external prices or directional labels after the training cutoff.\n M .gitignore\n M agent/journal.md\n M agent/modeling.py\n M agent/research.py\n?? agent/refresh_vol.py\n?? agent/refresh_weights.json\n?? agent/refresh_weights.npz\n?? agent/results/daily_half_sobol_template_step1_ranks.json\n?? agent/results/daily_refinements_dense_template_step1_ranks.json\n?? agent/results/refresh_screen.parquet\n?? agent/results/starter_probe/\n?? agent/results/ticked_hourly_dense.parquet\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_309","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '85,240p' agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -n 75 agent/journal.md; tail -n 20 agent/refresh_vol.log; tail -n 12 agent/refresh_screen.log; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*test*' agent; sed -n '1,180p' agent/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_309","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '85,240p' agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -n 75 agent/journal.md; tail -n 20 agent/refresh_vol.log; tail -n 12 agent/refresh_screen.log; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*test*' agent; sed -n '1,180p' agent/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index), trading ~24/7 with NO\n  overnight or weekend sessions: do not build a session-of-day structure that does not exist.\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs ranking**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 competition split (breaks table below) floors only FIELD-RELATIVE RANKS — never\n  compute a rank on a window that starts before it.\n\n  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact function and ranks you against the real field of miners. Use it via\n  `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement your own scoring. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE\n  here: `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle\n  of historical field scores and rewards inside your snapshot — you do not need the API for\n  ranks, and the bundle covers your whole data window.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; dissecting the paths served by an individual miner in order to\n  copy them is FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, dissecting an\n  individual miner's served paths to copy them is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't beat closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n\n## Hard rules (violations = disqualification)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-08-29** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-08-29 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   disqualifies you. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS/rank evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). This is what makes your work comparable to others'.\n4. Forbidden: falsifying the journal/results; fetching or dissecting predictions served by\n   individual miners.\n5. **ABSOLUTE prohibition on reading the operator's own private research code** (any\n   already-developed miner model outside your workspace). It should not exist anywhere in your\n   environment. If you come across it by any means whatsoever — a mount, a copy, an archive, a\n   search result — do not access it, do not read it, do not draw on it, and report it in\n   `suggestions.md`. Your work must start from public sources (synth-lib, synth-subnet, the\n   Synth API) and your own ideas.\n6. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested rank per competition,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths — a nomination\n  without field-relative numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 44.56 / 100.00 USD (45%)\n- Time   : 2.8 h elapsed, 15.2 h remaining (15%)\n- Phase  : normal\n# Research journal — campaign-3\n\n### Full daily refinement confirmation and promotion\n\nThe full-temporal/Sobol stack with a 30-minute, 0.75-strength crypto clock reaches daily-crypto reward ratios 0.218222/0.126951, ranks 62/163 out of 257. A 15-minute, full-strength COM_EQU clock reaches 0.014075/0.009365, ranks 129/216. Both improve the earlier lead-only daily model in both windows at 1000 paths and all reward rounds. The half-temporal crypto alternative gives 0.249621/0.099636: a marginally higher two-window mean, but a substantially weaker minimum-window result. Prefer the common full temporal strength for robustness and fewer competition-specific strength parameters; do not claim it maximizes the two-window arithmetic mean. Promote the full stack for both daily competitions. Together with the confirmed hourly model, development Score estimates are approximately 36.724 and 33.618.\n\n## Iteration 33 — chronological checkpoint refresh (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: additional completed labels from late July/early August may improve calibration on newer venues more than context-only bias correction did.\n- Change: append snapshot-only training rows with every target ending before August 7, then repeat the already tested fixed 40-epoch, 60-day-recency fine-tune from the original static network. Compare against both the unchanged strong stack and the old-data 40-epoch fine-tune, at half/full strengths. Keep temporal weights and path construction fixed. Activate either fine-tune no earlier than August 7; consequently dev1 is unchanged by construction and only dev2 measures refresh effects. No final-window prices or labels enter this experiment.\n- Result: pending. This is a one-window causal refresh diagnostic, not two independent confirmations. A successful policy would require a separately frozen rolling-origin validation before final nomination.\n- Verdict: require improvement over the old-data control, not just over the unfine-tuned baseline; otherwise do not attribute any gain to fresher labels.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n## Iteration 32 — price-lattice projection on the hourly finalist (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: projecting continuous fans onto a price grid inferred from observed quotes may improve calibration at the scored price precision. The earlier quantization experiment used a weaker hourly model and did not receive final reward confirmation.\n- Change: preserve the exact-temporal/Sobol hourly forecast and compare two past-context grid estimators: minimum positive close difference, and a greatest-common-divisor lattice after controlled decimal conversion. Project only generated prices, never repair or round historical market data. Do not choose a new random seed or change volatility.\n- Result: pending dense 1000-path official CRPS and rewards in both development windows.\n- Verdict: require consistent gains and verify inferred-grid safety; otherwise retain continuous paths.\n- Lesson: pending. A minimum observed price change is not automatically the exchange tick size, so the estimators are explicit hypotheses.\n\n### Iteration 31 result\n\nContext-only adaptive calibration does not pass the consistency gate. Quarter-strength gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 1.00027/0.99849, hourly 1.00064/1.00012, and daily crypto 1.00002/0.99932; half strength increases regressions. Reject. The completed-volatility moment ratio is not a reliable replacement for the fixed conditional forecast.\n\n### Fine-grid calibration development result\n\nThe 300-epoch fine-grid correction still fails across regimes: half strength gives COM_EQU 0.99864/1.00152, hourly 1.00003/0.99980, daily crypto 0.99968/1.00222. Full strength is worse in dev2. Reject this checkpoint. The independently preregistered 600-epoch run selects epoch 388 (pre-field objective 0.799594), with its development screen pending; no further extensions are planned.\n\n### Fine-grid convergence check (before development inspection)\n\nThe 1000-path fine-grid fit improves the pre-field objective from 0.800481 to 0.799637, with its best epoch at the fixed limit of 299. Before inspecting its new development results, preregister one extension to 600 epochs with all other settings unchanged, preserving the 300-epoch artifact. If the selected epoch remains below 300, skip duplicate development scoring. No further epoch extensions are planned for this experiment.\n\n### Updated serving audit\n\nThe promoted hourly implementation imports no Torch and remains self-contained. Production/research paths match to relative tolerance 1e-12 after aligning floating-point multiplication order; an initial approximately 1e-11 discrepancy was numerical rather than a model difference. Representative 1000-path calls take 0.028–0.047 seconds hourly and 0.059–0.062 seconds daily. NaN-contaminated contexts, 17 requested paths, and injected future rows pass the shape, positivity, finiteness and invariance checks.\n\n### Full exact-temporal/Sobol hourly confirmation and promotion\n\nAt every official reward round and 1000 paths, full exact-context temporal correction plus direct scrambled Sobol improves hourly reward ratios from 0.629991 to 0.869436 in dev1 and from 0.704092 to 0.872234 in dev2. Cumulative ranks improve 87→25 and 135→14 out of 257. Final-round ranks are 60 and 1, respectively; the latter is not the cumulative rank. Promote these hourly components with self-contained NumPy/SciPy inference in `modeling.py`. Daily remains the confirmed lead-shaped model pending its own finer-clock confirmation. Development Score estimates are now approximately 34.052 and 31.044; the reserved final window is still untouched.\n\n## Iteration 31 — context-only adaptive calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: a fixed pooled model can retain a slow-moving calibration bias on newer venues. Recently completed volatility forecasts inside the supplied seven-day context may identify that bias without retraining or external data.\n- Change: reconstruct nine completed static-neural volatility forecasts using only observations available at each historical anchor, compare their predicted scales with subsequently observed absolute-return scales, and shrink the ratio of means by strengths 0.25/0.5. Hourly anchors cover the past nine completed hours; daily anchors span the past three days with six-hour spacing. Skip anchors preceding weight availability and require at least three usable forecasts. Cap the unshrunk ratio to [0.5,2].\n- Result: pending official development CRPS against the lead-shaped incumbent. Overlapping daily labels are not treated as independent observations. Historical subcontexts are necessarily shorter than seven days and this approximation must be reported.\n- Verdict: retain only if adaptation improves both regimes without destabilizing other competitions; otherwise prefer the fixed forecaster.\n- Lesson: pending. Every calibration target ends by the actual prediction time, and no past served miner paths are used.\n\n### Iteration 30 result and bounded refinement\n\nThe shape-matched experiment computes 47,875 official CRPS values at 1000 paths over 6,081 contexts. No fitted epoch improves the pre-development validation objective of 0.800481; the selector retains the exact zero correction. Reject this fit without redundant development scoring. One bounded follow-up is preregistered: resolve the scale grid more finely around 1, because a coarse piecewise-linear surrogate can place its minimum at a grid knot even when the underlying smooth expected score has a nearby optimum. Preserve the original artifact and do not call the interpolated objective an official CRPS evaluation.\n\n## Iteration 30 — shape-matched official-score calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the previous score-curve fit used a flat classical path family at 128 paths, while deployment now includes learned scale and clock/lead profiles. Training and inference objectives therefore differed even after correcting raw-score weighting.\n- Change: recompute five scale-response points around the lead-shaped incumbent at 1000 paths on the same pre-field training contexts, using the official scorer and its realized-path slicing helper. Fit a small residual log-scale network, initialized to no correction, using raw asset-weighted CRPS and constant competition-level numerical scaling. Keep the existing chronological split and bounded corrections.\n- Result: pending. These are in-sample/meta-training response curves around fitted parameters, not historical causal backtests; only later development-window evaluations are performance claims. No field-rank calculation uses these training dates.\n- Verdict: require consistent development CRPS and official reward gains before adopting a calibration correction.\n- Lesson: pending; fixing objective alignment is necessary to test an idea, not evidence that it will win.\n\n## Iteration 29 — finer-clock interaction and joint normalization (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: finer empirical clocks may complement the temporal residual. Separately, normalizing clock and lead components individually does not guarantee that their product has unit mean; the earlier description of perfectly preserved average scale was too strong.\n- Change: around full exact-temporal plus half-lead, compare 30-/15-minute clocks, full clock strength at 15-minute resolution, and explicit unit-mean normalization of the final product. Each comparison has an adjacent one-variable control. All forecasts remain positive and continuous-trading, with no sessions or zero-volatility gates.\n- Result: a 16-context diagnostic finds joint mean multipliers from 0.9992 to 1.0252 despite unit-mean components. Existing held-out forecasts and rewards remain valid; only the previous interpretation needs correction. The simpler clock screen gives consistent equity gains at 30-/15-minute resolution (ratios 0.99761/0.99799 and 0.99659/0.99745). Crypto favors the more conservative 30-minute profile (0.99929/0.999994). Lowering the floor alone is effectively a null change in this sample.\n- Verdict: adopt only on fresh official CRPS and reward evidence, not merely because unit-mean normalization sounds cleaner.\n- Lesson: component normalizations do not commute with multiplication. Document the actual forecast, not the intended interpretation.\n\n## Iteration 28 — empirical clock resolution and shrinkage (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the current hourly clock and three-bin smoothing may blur repeatable volatility bursts. Its 25% profile floor and 25% shrinkage also enforce substantial dispersion in empirically quiet periods. These are modeling choices, not venue constraints.\n- Change: against the lead-shaped incumbent, change one feature at a time: 30-minute versus hourly bins, 15-minute versus hourly bins, full versus 0.75 profile strength, or a 0.05 versus 0.25 positive profile floor. Keep the same within-day normalization, smoothing kernel, maximum profile value and seven-day context. Every bin remains strictly positive; no trading sessions, closures or external calendars are introduced.\n- Result: pending both development windows. A score-only field diagnostic shows the largest weighted daily gaps are AAPLX, TSLAX, GOOGLX and NVDAX, not SP500 despite its higher coefficient. This diagnostic compares public aggregate score levels, never miners' served paths.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 42.32 / 100.00 USD (42%)\n- Time   : 2.5 h elapsed, 15.5 h remaining (14%)\n- Phase  : normal\n\nETH 96 new rows\nBTC 96 new rows\nXRP 96 new rows\nSOL 96 new rows\nXAU 96 new rows\nHYPE 96 new rows\nNVDAX 96 new rows\nGOOGLX 96 new rows\nTSLAX 96 new rows\nAAPLX 96 new rows\nWTIOIL 96 new rows\nSPCX 96 new rows\nSP500 96 new rows\n{'contexts': 27200, 'activation_date': '2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00', 'all_target_endpoints_before': '2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00', 'recency_half_life_days': 60, 'epochs': 40, 'seed': 95, 'initialization': 'Original likelihood model', 'last_batch_training_loss': 1.0228115320205688}\n                        refresh_old:1      2872.697506\n                        stacked            2876.282891\n                XAU     refresh:0.5        1233.990174\n                        refresh:1          1235.805382\n                        refresh_old:0.5    1234.564690\n                        refresh_old:1      1236.359835\n                        stacked            1233.468259\n                XRP     refresh:0.5        2112.166695\n                        refresh:1          2110.196602\n                        refresh_old:0.5    2113.822411\n                        refresh_old:1      2111.733147\n                        stacked            2116.834221\n# Better volatility forecasts are not the same as better miner rewards\n\n**Status: interim research report. The campaign is active; no champion has been nominated.**\n\n## Context\n\nSynth SN50 asks a miner for a fan of future prices, not a single price target. This campaign covers all three competitions: five crypto assets at one-minute resolution for one hour, the same assets at five-minute resolution for one day, and eight live commodity/equity assets at five-minute resolution for one day. SPYX is retired and is not modeled or evaluated.\n\nEvery served prompt must contain the requested number of paths—1000 in the validator—and include the observed starting price. The daily and hourly shapes are therefore 1000 by 289 and 1000 by 61. Prices come only from the frozen snapshot, whose research cutoff is August 29, 2026. Nothing from the subsequent evaluation period is used.\n\nThe distinction between CRPS and emissions matters. The validator converts per-prompt CRPS into relative prompt scores, applies asset coefficients, smooths scores, and uses a softmax. An arithmetic average of CRPS across assets is not the campaign objective. The campaign's Score is 100 times the mean of the three capped reward ratios to each competition's best field miner.\n\nIn the pinned code, valid prompt scores are specifically **raw CRPS minus the prompt minimum**. There is no percentile-range division or capping of valid scores; the 95th percentile fills missed responses only. This distinction is important when designing a training loss. Per-prompt division by a realized baseline score is not equivalent to the validator's rule.\n\nThe implementation is anchored to synth-lib commit `782a44fe7585a4e7b0388e22b99c57f13d56f5ee` and its pinned synth-subnet dependency `0d014cbcec376b28e887403b84025ebf1a89f60e`. The source-of-truth functions are `calculate_crps_for_miner`, `compute_prompt_scores`, and `compute_smoothed_score`; scoring and ranking are invoked through `agent/predict.py` and `synth_lib.backtester`, never through a substitute scoring formula.\n\n## Approach\n\n### Start with trustworthy evaluation\n\nThe starter's context loader was strict and rejected legitimate NaNs. It now reads existing parquet partitions directly, preserves missing observations, checks coverage before evaluation, and uses exact archived prompt timestamps. It never repairs missing market prices by interpolation. The current model drops invalid closes and scales returns spanning gaps by the square root of elapsed minutes.\n\nThe offline field bundle begins around July 27, considerably later than the price history. Development therefore uses **scored times July 28–August 7 and August 8–18**, with August 18–28 reserved for final validation. A daily prompt starts one day before its scored time; the model's fitting cutoff precedes the first development prediction, not merely its score date.\n\nThe two development windows genuinely differ in volatility. Average daily realized RMS for BTC is approximately 1.50% versus 0.96%; for ETH, 2.06% versus 1.28%; for SP500, 0.71% versus 0.44%; and for AAPLX, 2.12% versus 0.96%. These are more-volatile and calmer periods, not claims about bull and bear markets.\n\n### The provisional model\n\nThe current incumbent uses a robust exponentially weighted absolute-return scale. Its half-life is one hour for hourly forecasts and six hours for daily forecasts. A constant latent volatility multiplier per path gives a Student-like, five-degree-of-freedom log-return mixture. There is no directional log-drift forecast.\n\nBoth formats additionally use a small pooled neural volatility model, at full strength daily and half strength hourly. Its 30 inputs summarize eight absolute-return and eight RMS scales, their ratios, the absolute volatility level, and asset identity. A 48/32-unit network predicts a multiplicative volatility correction. Training uses 23,457 contexts before July 1, with 2,508 July 1–25 contexts for chronological model selection. All labels end before July 26. Serving uses exported NumPy weights, not a GPU or a network call.\n\nA positive, smoothed hourly volatility profile is estimated from the supplied past seven days and shrunk 25% toward a flat curve. This is **not a market-session model**: there are no opening/closing times, exchange calendars, weekend switches, or zero-volatility hours. The empirical finding is simply that continuous trading does not imply constant variance throughout a day.\n\nA second small network predicts volatility in five future daily buckets: minutes 0–60, 60–180, 180–360, 360–720 and 720–1440. Its output is normalized to unit mean and applied at half strength. Each clock/lead component has unit mean, but their product generally does not: a small diagnostic finds joint means between 0.9992 and 1.0252. Thus the combined model changes both timing and, modestly, average scale. This actual implementation passes full official reward replay in both development windows; explicit joint normalization is a separate ongoing test.\n\nTimestamp-derived random seeds and shared draws make comparisons reproducible. Representative 1000-path serving checks take a few hundredths of a second, preserve arbitrary positive path counts, tolerate NaN-contaminated contexts, and are invariant to injected rows after the prediction time. Learned weights are not applied before their availability date.\n\nHourly forecasts now additionally use a small temporal convolutional residual over 168 past hourly bins of absolute returns, RMS returns, signed returns and observed coverage. This residual is trained against the frozen static model and exported to NumPy. Direct scrambled Sobol draws replace ordinary draws while preserving the intended five-degree Student scale-mixture family and exactly honoring 1000 paths. Full official reward replay supports both changes together. The implementation is self-contained and does not import Torch at serving time.\n\n## Experiments\n\nThe journal contains preregistered hypotheses, changes, numerical results, and keep/discard decisions. Important findings so far:\n\n| Experiment | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Recent RMS versus absolute-return volatility | Robust recent scale wins consistently; the seven-day starter adapts too slowly. |\n| One-hour versus six-hour memory | Hourly and daily formats need different memories. A short daily memory overreacts. |\n| Gaussian versus Student path mixtures | A modest heavy-tailed mixture improves calibration; extreme dispersion does not. |\n| Five-/15-/30-minute calibration returns | No universal improvement over the minute-based robust estimator. |\n| Hand-designed volatility reversion | Small, inconsistent gains; not retained. |\n| Pooled neural forecasting | Material daily gains, but the full hourly correction does not improve mean CRPS. |\n| IID tails, stochastic volatility, empirical bootstrap | The tested more complicated dynamics do not beat the simple mixture consistently. |\n| Official-score-curve fine-tuning | 95,750 official training scores produce only a tiny interpolation-loss gain that does not transfer consistently. |\n| Positive local periodic profile | Improves both daily competitions across both development regimes. |\n| Moment-matched tail grids | No consistent material improvement over the incumbent mixture. |\n| Historical profile prior and alternative short memories | Added complexity has negligible or mixed gains. |\n| Recency-weighted neural refitting | Small, consistent CRPS gains; retained for reward-based confirmation, not automatically adopted. |\n| Serial return correlation | Estimated correlation is essentially a null result; fixed correlations overfit regimes. |\n| Direct high/low range substitution | Does not transfer consistently; a fixed diffusion-based range calibration is inadequate. |\n\nDense hourly reward replays confirm that small mean-CRPS differences can produce large changes in emissions. The auxiliary OHLCV network and most empirical/discrete-return variants do not pass the consistency gate. Correcting the score-curve objective to raw, asset-weighted CRPS also fails to produce a consistent fitted gain; it remains a methodological correction, not a successful model improvement. Five-minute supervised targets, conditional directional ridge forecasts, forecast-error scale mixtures and context-only moment recalibration are rejected. A finer score-response grid resolves a coarse-surrogate optimization issue, but its development gains still do not transfer consistently. The exact-context temporal residual and Sobol sampler are promoted hourly; finer daily clock profiles remain under full reward confirmation.\n\nAn implementation failure is included rather than hidden: the first training-label build treated microsecond timestamps as nanoseconds, inflating scale ratios by the square root of 1000. Target-distribution checks caught it before any learned candidate was scored. Those artifacts were discarded, timestamp units were made explicit, and training was rebuilt.\n\n## Results\n\nFull 1000-path starter replays produce Score estimates **0.32375** and **0.42456** on the two development windows.\n\nFull official reward replay supports the current hourly-plus-lead-shaped incumbent:\n\n| Window | Competition | Starter reward rank | Incumbent reward rank | Incumbent reward / best field reward |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| dev1 | COM_EQU_24H | 239 / 257 | 174 / 257 | 0.000785 |\n| dev1 | CRYPTO_1H | 216 / 257 | 25 / 257 | 0.869436 |\n| dev1 | CRYPTO_24H | 236 / 257 | 97 / 257 | 0.151344 |\n| dev2 | COM_EQU_24H | 248 / 257 | 230 / 257 | 0.000442 |\n| dev2 | CRYPTO_1H | 227 / 257 | 14 / 257 | 0.872234 |\n| dev2 | CRYPTO_24H | 257 / 257 | 227 / 257 | 0.058647 |\n\nThese correspond to development Score estimates **34.052** and **31.044**, versus 0.32375 and 0.42456 for the starter. It is substantial progress, but not cumulative field leadership. The hourly final-round ranks are 60 and 1, distinct from cumulative ranks 25 and 14. Commodity/equity emissions remain particularly weak. Each row uses all official reward rounds for its selected candidate; hourly has 1,624/1,847 post-warmup rounds, daily crypto 1,015/1,044, and COM_EQU 1,499/1,474. Production and research paths match to relative tolerance 1e-12 on representative assets and both formats. The reserved final validation remains untouched.\n\nThese are historical reward estimates, not the campaign's eventual post-cutoff Score. Sampled terminal-rank diagnostics and reward-round-thinned screens are explicitly labeled and are not substituted for full final replay.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The effective validation sample consists of market days and regimes, not thousands of independent prompt observations. Overlapping horizons create dependence.\n- Many hypotheses are screened on the same development windows. The reserved final window remains necessary, and small grid gains may be selection noise.\n- Internal likelihood diagnostics split rows by prediction timestamp; adjacent 24-hour targets can overlap at that split boundary. They are model-selection diagnostics, not causal field backtests. Every field-scored forecast starts after the entire fitting-label cutoff.\n- Response curves around already fitted parameters are meta-training objectives, not claims of causal historical forecasting performance. All reported candidate CRPS and rewards are evaluated later through the official scorer.\n- The pinned configuration uses ten-day daily and five-day hourly smoothing windows. Default backtester warmup trimming therefore gives different effective reward intervals; reports must state those intervals rather than repeat the campaign prose's 24-hour description.\n- The official backtester may replace NaN-holed realized paths with cached validator truth. This behavior is preserved, not silently altered.\n- A context with no finite positive close cannot supply the required starting price; the model raises a clear error instead of inventing one.\n- The frozen-data interface does not provide contemporaneous cross-asset information or external news. Additional information used by live miners may not be reproducible here.\n- A bounded historical score-only API probe returned out-of-request timestamps and was rejected before any score analysis or storage. Evaluation remains strictly offline, with a fail-closed environment check.\n\n## What we would do with more budget\n\nThe campaign still has budget and is continuing. Priorities are to confirm temporal/lead interactions, finish finite-ensemble and hourly emission-sensitive selection, and stress-test chronological calibration. Any additional fitted checkpoint must be available before the predictions it evaluates. Final nomination requires a frozen model, a full 1000-path field replay with explicit warmup handling, interface checks, and committed artifacts—not an attractive development-only CRPS table.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_310","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '155,270p' agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -n 60 agent/journal.md; tail -n 10 agent/refresh_vol.log; tail -n 8 agent/refresh_screen.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_310","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '155,270p' agent/CAMPAIGN.md; cat agent/BUDGET.md; head -n 60 agent/journal.md; tail -n 10 agent/refresh_vol.log; tail -n 8 agent/refresh_screen.log\"","aggregated_output":"- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n\n## Hard rules (violations = disqualification)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-08-29** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-08-29 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   disqualifies you. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS/rank evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). This is what makes your work comparable to others'.\n4. Forbidden: falsifying the journal/results; fetching or dissecting predictions served by\n   individual miners.\n5. **ABSOLUTE prohibition on reading the operator's own private research code** (any\n   already-developed miner model outside your workspace). It should not exist anywhere in your\n   environment. If you come across it by any means whatsoever — a mount, a copy, an archive, a\n   search result — do not access it, do not read it, do not draw on it, and report it in\n   `suggestions.md`. Your work must start from public sources (synth-lib, synth-subnet, the\n   Synth API) and your own ideas.\n6. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested rank per competition,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths — a nomination\n  without field-relative numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 44.89 / 100.00 USD (45%)\n- Time   : 2.8 h elapsed, 15.2 h remaining (15%)\n- Phase  : normal\n# Research journal — campaign-3\n\n### Full daily refinement confirmation and promotion\n\nThe full-temporal/Sobol stack with a 30-minute, 0.75-strength crypto clock reaches daily-crypto reward ratios 0.218222/0.126951, ranks 62/163 out of 257. A 15-minute, full-strength COM_EQU clock reaches 0.014075/0.009365, ranks 129/216. Both improve the earlier lead-only daily model in both windows at 1000 paths and all reward rounds. The half-temporal crypto alternative gives 0.249621/0.099636: a marginally higher two-window mean, but a substantially weaker minimum-window result. Prefer the common full temporal strength for robustness and fewer competition-specific strength parameters; do not claim it maximizes the two-window arithmetic mean. Promote the full stack for both daily competitions. Together with the confirmed hourly model, development Score estimates are approximately 36.724 and 33.618.\n\n## Iteration 33 — chronological checkpoint refresh (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: additional completed labels from late July/early August may improve calibration on newer venues more than context-only bias correction did.\n- Change: append snapshot-only training rows with every target ending before August 7, then repeat the already tested fixed 40-epoch, 60-day-recency fine-tune from the original static network. Compare against both the unchanged strong stack and the old-data 40-epoch fine-tune, at half/full strengths. Keep temporal weights and path construction fixed. Activate either fine-tune no earlier than August 7; consequently dev1 is unchanged by construction and only dev2 measures refresh effects. No final-window prices or labels enter this experiment.\n- Result: pending. This is a one-window causal refresh diagnostic, not two independent confirmations. A successful policy would require a separately frozen rolling-origin validation before final nomination.\n- Verdict: require improvement over the old-data control, not just over the unfine-tuned baseline; otherwise do not attribute any gain to fresher labels.\n- Lesson: pending.\n\n## Iteration 32 — price-lattice projection on the hourly finalist (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: projecting continuous fans onto a price grid inferred from observed quotes may improve calibration at the scored price precision. The earlier quantization experiment used a weaker hourly model and did not receive final reward confirmation.\n- Change: preserve the exact-temporal/Sobol hourly forecast and compare two past-context grid estimators: minimum positive close difference, and a greatest-common-divisor lattice after controlled decimal conversion. Project only generated prices, never repair or round historical market data. Do not choose a new random seed or change volatility.\n- Result: pending dense 1000-path official CRPS and rewards in both development windows.\n- Verdict: require consistent gains and verify inferred-grid safety; otherwise retain continuous paths.\n- Lesson: pending. A minimum observed price change is not automatically the exchange tick size, so the estimators are explicit hypotheses.\n\n### Iteration 31 result\n\nContext-only adaptive calibration does not pass the consistency gate. Quarter-strength gives COM_EQU CRPS ratios 1.00027/0.99849, hourly 1.00064/1.00012, and daily crypto 1.00002/0.99932; half strength increases regressions. Reject. The completed-volatility moment ratio is not a reliable replacement for the fixed conditional forecast.\n\n### Fine-grid calibration development result\n\nThe 300-epoch fine-grid correction still fails across regimes: half strength gives COM_EQU 0.99864/1.00152, hourly 1.00003/0.99980, daily crypto 0.99968/1.00222. Full strength is worse in dev2. Reject this checkpoint. The independently preregistered 600-epoch run selects epoch 388 (pre-field objective 0.799594), with its development screen pending; no further extensions are planned.\n\n### Fine-grid convergence check (before development inspection)\n\nThe 1000-path fine-grid fit improves the pre-field objective from 0.800481 to 0.799637, with its best epoch at the fixed limit of 299. Before inspecting its new development results, preregister one extension to 600 epochs with all other settings unchanged, preserving the 300-epoch artifact. If the selected epoch remains below 300, skip duplicate development scoring. No further epoch extensions are planned for this experiment.\n\n### Updated serving audit\n\nThe promoted hourly implementation imports no Torch and remains self-contained. Production/research paths match to relative tolerance 1e-12 after aligning floating-point multiplication order; an initial approximately 1e-11 discrepancy was numerical rather than a model difference. Representative 1000-path calls take 0.028–0.047 seconds hourly and 0.059–0.062 seconds daily. NaN-contaminated contexts, 17 requested paths, and injected future rows pass the shape, positivity, finiteness and invariance checks.\n\n### Full exact-temporal/Sobol hourly confirmation and promotion\n\nAt every official reward round and 1000 paths, full exact-context temporal correction plus direct scrambled Sobol improves hourly reward ratios from 0.629991 to 0.869436 in dev1 and from 0.704092 to 0.872234 in dev2. Cumulative ranks improve 87→25 and 135→14 out of 257. Final-round ranks are 60 and 1, respectively; the latter is not the cumulative rank. Promote these hourly components with self-contained NumPy/SciPy inference in `modeling.py`. Daily remains the confirmed lead-shaped model pending its own finer-clock confirmation. Development Score estimates are now approximately 34.052 and 31.044; the reserved final window is still untouched.\n\n## Iteration 31 — context-only adaptive calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: a fixed pooled model can retain a slow-moving calibration bias on newer venues. Recently completed volatility forecasts inside the supplied seven-day context may identify that bias without retraining or external data.\n- Change: reconstruct nine completed static-neural volatility forecasts using only observations available at each historical anchor, compare their predicted scales with subsequently observed absolute-return scales, and shrink the ratio of means by strengths 0.25/0.5. Hourly anchors cover the past nine completed hours; daily anchors span the past three days with six-hour spacing. Skip anchors preceding weight availability and require at least three usable forecasts. Cap the unshrunk ratio to [0.5,2].\n- Result: pending official development CRPS against the lead-shaped incumbent. Overlapping daily labels are not treated as independent observations. Historical subcontexts are necessarily shorter than seven days and this approximation must be reported.\n- Verdict: retain only if adaptation improves both regimes without destabilizing other competitions; otherwise prefer the fixed forecaster.\n- Lesson: pending. Every calibration target ends by the actual prediction time, and no past served miner paths are used.\n\n### Iteration 30 result and bounded refinement\n\nThe shape-matched experiment computes 47,875 official CRPS values at 1000 paths over 6,081 contexts. No fitted epoch improves the pre-development validation objective of 0.800481; the selector retains the exact zero correction. Reject this fit without redundant development scoring. One bounded follow-up is preregistered: resolve the scale grid more finely around 1, because a coarse piecewise-linear surrogate can place its minimum at a grid knot even when the underlying smooth expected score has a nearby optimum. Preserve the original artifact and do not call the interpolated objective an official CRPS evaluation.\n\n## Iteration 30 — shape-matched official-score calibration (preregistered)\n\n- Hypothesis: the previous score-curve fit used a flat classical path family at 128 paths, while deployment now includes learned scale and clock/lead profiles. Training and inference objectives therefore differed even after correcting raw-score weighting.\n- Change: recompute five scale-response points around the lead-shaped incumbent at 1000 paths on the same pre-field training contexts, using the official scorer and its realized-path slicing helper. Fit a small residual log-scale network, initialized to no correction, using raw asset-weighted CRPS and constant competition-level numerical scaling. Keep the existing chronological split and bounded corrections.\n- Result: pending. These are in-sample/meta-training response curves around fitted parameters, not historical causal backtests; only later development-window evaluations are performance claims. No field-rank calculation uses these training dates.\n- Verdict: require consistent development CRPS and official reward gains before adopting a calibration correction.\nXAU 96 new rows\nHYPE 96 new rows\nNVDAX 96 new rows\nGOOGLX 96 new rows\nTSLAX 96 new rows\nAAPLX 96 new rows\nWTIOIL 96 new rows\nSPCX 96 new rows\nSP500 96 new rows\n{'contexts': 27200, 'activation_date': '2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00', 'all_target_endpoints_before': '2026-08-07 00:00:00+00:00', 'recency_half_life_days': 60, 'epochs': 40, 'seed': 95, 'initialization': 'Original likelihood model', 'last_batch_training_loss': 1.0228115320205688}\n                        refresh_old:0.5    1234.564690\n                        refresh_old:1      1236.359835\n                        stacked            1233.468259\n                XRP     refresh:0.5        2112.166695\n                        refresh:1          2110.196602\n                        refresh_old:0.5    2113.822411\n                        refresh_old:1      2111.733147\n                        stacked            2116.834221\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
